WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.390
Humans have always been explorers.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:13.060
As a species, we are innately curious about
what lies beyond our horizon.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:19.380
If the day ever comes when we embark across
the cosmic oceans beyond our moon, what vessels

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.480
will we use to discover new worlds?

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.259
Few art projects capture the wonder and awe
of voyaging to an unfamiliar planet better

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:31.720
than the ‘Explorer’ series.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:37.970
Brought to life by incredible digital artist
Christian Grajewski, the story is set in a

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:44.020
distant future, where the remnants of humanity
journey to a lush exoplanet called Aion.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:50.051
The ongoing ‘Explorer’ project doesn’t
just examine a vibrant alien ecosystem, but

00:00:50.051 --> 00:00:55.900
also documents the fascinating vehicles the
early explorers use to chart the unfamiliar

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:56.900
planet.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.940
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
tag along with the first wave of explorers,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.160
and voyage across this mysterious world in
a range of different machines.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:12.620
And as always, you can follow and support
the artist using the links in the description.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:19.400
Now, let’s set off towards Planet Aion…

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.730
We set out from Planet Earth on the Ourea
— the greatest construction project in human

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:25.830
history.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:31.100
And after a brief journey of three-hundred
and seventeen years, we arrive.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:38.110
To explore planet Aion up close, we must leave
behind the safety of the mothership.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.640
Landing on the planet’s surface, for the
first stage of our expedition, we’ll be

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.659
traveling in the Ant, a compact research vehicle
that will make exploring Planet Aion safer

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:51.230
and more efficient.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:56.610
Setting off from our landing site, it’s
not long before we spot our first lifeform.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:01.350
Stooping for a drink in a shallow creek are
unique, bipedal herbivores.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:08.280
They’re tall — over two and a half meters,
or nine feet, and have an elegant appearance.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.540
Like earth deer, they seem rather skittish
— bounding across the terrain as we pass

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.540
by.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:20.400
A few of them stand their ground, not interested
in abandoning their prime watering hole.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.709
Using their two lower horns, they begin to
produce a rattling noise that seems intended

00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:29.480
to frighten away predators.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.709
But perhaps it isn’t us that these deer
are scared of.

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:38.099
Emerging from the underbrush are the first
predators we’ve seen on this planet.

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:42.299
These are the Silent Death, and they are built
for the chase.

00:02:42.299 --> 00:02:48.459
Like cheetahs, their aerodynamic bodies are
capable of incredible bursts of speed.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:55.900
Similar to many lifeforms on Aion, the Silent
Death lack eyes, instead relying on sonar-like

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:56.900
echolocation.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.760
Soon, a chase will begin between predator
and prey — a drama known to most planets

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.760
with complex life...

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:12.780
Driving through a marshy swampland, we can
spot a rather singular looking lifeform.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:18.360
These bizarre herbivores are a member of the
Formaminis Caput classification — a taxon

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.090
marked by the sizable holes in the middle
of their skull.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:27.069
Getting a closer look at this alarming gap,
we can see the hole serves as an enormous

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:33.260
olfactory organ — with sensitive hairs giving
the creature an incredible sense of smell.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.680
An extreme — yet effective — anatomical
feature.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.879
With their long snouts and tongues, this species
somewhat resembles an anteater.

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:47.909
The exact function of their tongues isn’t
known, but likely helps them snag their preferred

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:51.049
food source from the swampy water.

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:56.819
Yet in this marshland, sticking your tongue
in the water can be dangerous.

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:02.760
From the murky depths, a menacing predator
lifts themselves on two muscular arms.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:08.920
With a length of eleven meters, or thirty-six
feet, this semi-aquatic lifeform is the largest

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.519
carnivore we’ve seen so far.

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:16.930
Like the alligators or crocodiles of Earth,
these patient hunters ambush their prey from

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.510
the water — drowning them or swallowing
them whole.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.690
Their snake-like bodies make them excellent
swimmers, and their strong arms paired with

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:30.759
their spring-like musculature allows for explosive
movements.

00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:35.580
Passing near the water’s edge is a risky
venture...

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:41.220
Yet one titan in this watering hole pays little
mind to what lurks below the surface.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.419
This is a Stag-Beetle Rhino, and they spend
their days combing through the swamp in search

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.480
of tasty vegetation.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.680
Curiously, their breathing organ is at the
highest point of the tail, helping them stay

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.789
underwater for longer.

00:04:55.789 --> 00:05:00.880
Strange as that might sound, various Earth
beetles breathe through spiracles in the rear

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.789
of their abdomen.

00:05:02.789 --> 00:05:07.810
When plodding through the swamp, Stag Beetle
Rhinos use two antennae on their backs to

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:12.340
create whip-like sounds that warn predators
to keep their distance.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.270
For no species wants to end up on the receiving
end of their horn…

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:22.300
For the next stage of our tour, we’ll need
to travel in something that can handle all

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.950
manner of terrain.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:29.259
The Monkey is a futuristic research vehicle
suitable for the most challenging environments,

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:33.689
and will take us into the teeming forests
of this alien world.

00:05:33.689 --> 00:05:39.580
And speaking of monkeys, in the branches above
us are the Aion equivalent of primates.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:46.160
The Odellion is a somewhat odd-looking herbivore
that feeds on the foliage of the high canopy.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:51.720
Like the woolly monkeys of the amazon rainforest,
moving from branch to branch is second nature

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.240
to the Odellion.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:58.379
This field sketch shows the Odellion’s method
of traversing the canopy — a locomotive

00:05:58.379 --> 00:06:02.870
strategy that might look backwards to us,
but serves them well.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:09.220
Curiously, the Odellion seem to lack teeth
altogether, instead opting for long, muscular

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:15.590
lips — a trait that helps them pluck leaves
off the high branches.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:21.330
Deeper in the forest, from the bank of a forest
stream, a brightly-hued creature is watching

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:22.330
us.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:27.340
This is a Toxicorum — a mid-sized predator
with highly poisonous skin.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:33.610
Like the vivid colors of a sea slug, the Toxicorum’s
coloration is aposematic — warning other

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.770
predators against taking a bite.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:41.800
And if a Toxicorum subdues their prey, they
drink their victim’s internal liquid using

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:42.800
their tentacles.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.599
We’re fortunate to be behind protective
glass...

00:06:47.599 --> 00:06:52.370
Among the flooded roots of the forest is an
exceedingly rare gathering.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:57.470
These bizarre creatures are Magna Caputs — and
there are just a few thousand of them left

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:58.599
on this planet.

00:06:58.599 --> 00:07:04.310
Their giant heads can make a deep drumming
sound when threatened, and house highly sensitive

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:06.120
receptive organs.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:11.490
Typically solitary, the Magna Caputs will
call out to each other over thousands of kilometers

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.710
during breeding season, and come to congregate
in forests like this one.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:19.580
We’re lucky to be able to observe such an
infrequent ceremony.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:24.110
But it’s best to give them some privacy…

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:30.169
In a dense region of the forest, a standoffish
looking creature blocks the path of our vehicle.

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:35.460
This is a Turtleneck, a solitary lifeform
that — despite being an herbivore — is

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.379
an aggressive bully that doesn’t tolerate
intruders.

00:07:39.379 --> 00:07:44.439
Like rhinos, Turtlenecks will charge any lifeform
they deem a trespasser.

00:07:44.439 --> 00:07:49.840
Two shield-like protrusions protect the sides
of their body — and seem to envelop their

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:50.840
heads.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:56.009
These shields can change from soft and flexible
to hard and rigid in an instant, and allow

00:07:56.009 --> 00:08:00.780
the Turtleneck to ram into their targets at
high speeds without risk of injury.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:06.849
Our best move is to take the long way around
the Turtlenecks’ territory.

00:08:06.849 --> 00:08:13.639
In a nearby clearing, a small flock of bipedal
omnivores hiss at our vehicle as it passes.

00:08:13.639 --> 00:08:18.700
These pint-sized creatures resemble alien
chickens, although instead of a beak, they

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:24.449
possess a muscular hole for a mouth surrounded
by four frenzied tentacles.

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:28.949
Like Earth shorebirds, these generalists scavenge
often for food.

00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:34.560
They’ve become unexpectedly fast little
creatures in pursuit of small insects that

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.030
are the alien chicken’s favorite food, which
this field sketch illustrates.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:45.420
To aid in this endeavor, their tentacles are
sticky and act like flyswatters, bringing

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:50.140
insects directly into their gullet…

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:56.010
To chart the next region, we’ll be traversing
in Crickets — lightweight helicopters capable

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.750
of vertical take-off and landing.

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:04.820
Try to stay close, for we’re passing through
an area home to the largest megafauna on this

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:06.150
planet.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:10.540
Looking up at us as we pass overhead is a
herd of Bone Heads.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:15.660
These bellowing behemoths possess a triangular
hole in the middle of their head that serves

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:22.150
as an olfactory organ, marking them as another
member of the Foraminis caput classification.

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:27.850
Like hippos, the Bone Heads have evolved to
spend most of their time partially submerged

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:31.830
in water, which helps support their significant
bulk.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:36.630
Connected to the Bone Heads’ hollow skulls
are flexible, fishing rod-like trunks they

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.330
use to catch aquatic lifeforms.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:45.500
For the Bone Heads are omnivores, feeding
on all manner of underwater organisms to stay

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.540
satiated.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:53.710
Turning westward, we can spot a noisy procession
of Circulus.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:59.800
Another type of Foraminis caput, their circular
head gives them the ability to make a unique,

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:01.130
loud call.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:06.430
The distorted shape of their skull amplifies
the noise — although the result is a face

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.580
that looks rather unusual by our standards.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:15.400
The Circulus also possess two tentacles that
they use for smelling, navigating their environment

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.840
with their keen olfactory senses…

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:25.780
Not far from the Circulus procession, a group
of clumsy and colorful looking bipeds slurp

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:27.700
from the muddy water.

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:33.010
These are Anguis Collums, and they are on
the small side for this region — at about

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.500
one meter, or three feet.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:40.800
These omnivores might be unassuming, but they
feed in a somewhat unsettling manner.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:47.830
As this field sketch shows, the Anguis Collums
use their long tongue to dig into mussel-like

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:52.770
creatures, then blow poison into them and
wait until their soft tissue becomes liquid,

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:53.770
which they then drink.

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:59.910
A disquieting way to get a meal, although
nature is often unforgiving...

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:05.220
Snow and harsh winds block our path to the
north, so we’ll hitch a brief ride in the

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:12.450
massive Frog — a mobile research lab that
moves on wheels over fourteen meters, or forty-five

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:13.650
feet high.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.240
In a vehicle like this, we’re ready for
anything.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:21.660
And it seems we’ve attracted the attention
of something equally massive.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:27.120
This inquisitive giant is called a Gantor,
and they seem puzzled — but not particularly

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.270
bothered — by our presence here.

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:35.430
At nearly twenty-five meters, or eighty-two
feet in length, Gantors are an incredible

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:38.020
species to witness up close.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:43.690
Yet even more impressive than the Gantors’
sheer size is their remarkable intelligence.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:50.830
Gantors possess two trunk-like appendages
that play an important role in their communication.

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:55.920
During mating season, they seem to use their
tongue for some kind of sign-language, to

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:59.250
impress and “talk” to their favored partner.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:04.850
Exactly how intelligent Gantors are is difficult
to ascertain, but it’s possible we’ve

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:09.730
just made first contact with something very
special...

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:15.450
To venture further out into the unknown, we’ll
be taking to the skies once again in lightweight

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:17.460
aircrafts called Geese.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:23.800
Don’t let their unusual shape fool you — these
crafts are fast and agile enough to get us

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:29.550
an up-close look at the rarest lifeforms.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:35.880
Trumpeting at us while we pass overhead is
a herd of what appears to be alien elephants.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:41.830
Yet what seem to be large ears from a side-view
are actually massive bone structure surrounded

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:43.580
by soft muscle tissue.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:49.630
Their unusual skull also contains a large
hole — marking them as another member of

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:52.780
the Foraminis Caput classification.

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:58.500
Like the Circulus, this unique head acts as
a powerful sound amplifier for the thunderous

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:04.670
calls of these alien elephants — calls they
use to connect over vast distances.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:09.390
For unlike the social elephants of Earth,
these curious creatures spend much of their

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:13.760
lifecycle all alone...

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:18.850
Landing for a moment to stretch our legs,
we make an unsettling discovery.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:24.770
Not far from our position are three Citus
Mortems — the most dangerous hypercarnivores

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.330
on planet Aion.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:31.740
The aerodynamic shape of these terrors allows
them to make tight turns when pursuing a target

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.480
— and reach blazing speeds.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:40.000
Like Earth tigers, their efficient musculature
helps them take down prey many times larger

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:41.410
than they are.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:45.470
Yet unlike tigers, these predators hunt in
coordinated packs.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:51.830
And with the strongest bite force on the planet,
the Citus Mortems have no natural enemies.

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:58.920
Our best move now is to get back in our vehicles
and fly in the opposite direction.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:04.770
Back in the skies once more, we pass over
a valley home to the single largest land animals

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.260
on this planet.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:13.330
This is a herd of Tortugantus, creatures that
from tip to tail are over one hundred meters,

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:16.000
or three-hundred feet in length.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:21.560
Witnessing a Tortugantus is like seeing a
god walk upon the soil of a planet.

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:27.710
With such massive bodies, the Tortugantus
herd spends most of their days finding food,

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:30.210
mainly in the form of vegetation.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:36.010
Herds like this one can contain up to fifty
individuals, and are remarkably close knit

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:44.350
— as the intelligent Tortugantus are capable
of feeling empathy for their fellow herd members…

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:50.020
For the final and most spectacular leg of
our journey, we’ll be traveling in the top-of-the-line

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:51.220
Hornets.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:56.520
These vessels can travel vast distances without
stopping — which is important, for we’ll

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:59.640
be flying over the planet’s ocean.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:06.330
On the rocky coast, a somewhat unusual group
pays little attention to our passing crafts.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:12.050
These are Snail Frogs — agile and rather
playful omnivorous creatures.

00:15:12.050 --> 00:15:17.560
Their upper and lower body have sail-like
structures — which seems to be used to make

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:23.680
the Snail Frogs look bigger to predators,
and to impress fellow members of their species.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:30.530
Like Earth snails, Snail Frog stalk eyes are
capable of 360-degree rotation, and they give

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:35.270
the species a distinctive and somewhat amusing
looking appearance.

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:40.710
Snail Frogs primarily feed on mollusk like
creatures in shallow tidepools, which they

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:47.040
scratch from the rocks using their powerful,
four-part jaws...

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:53.980
Moving out to sea, we can spot our first fully-aquatic
lifeforms swimming just under the surface.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:59.250
Chasing a group of fish-analogues are the
Triangulus Stingray — pursuit predators

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:02.060
with slim, aerodynamic bodies.

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:06.910
Like Earth Stingrays, they possess barbs capable
of producing venom.

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:11.990
Yet the Triangulus Stingray’s possess three
forward-facing barbs instead of one in the

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:15.300
rear — which they use to spear their prey.

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:21.000
With their triangular wing shape, these hunters
can stop almost immediately, or change direction

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.730
when pursuing a meal…

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:30.000
Further out to sea, a larger, more terrifying
creature dwells in the depths.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:35.670
Up to eleven meters, or thirty-five feet in
length, these predators prefer to hunt in

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:38.390
the colder reaches of planet Aion.

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:43.290
Nicknamed Alien Seals, these lifeforms are
swift in the water.

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:47.980
Like Earth seals, however, when they come
on to land, they can’t do much but flop

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:48.980
about.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:53.720
Some say these alien seals look quite peaceful
when lying on the shore — although this

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:58.630
sight is difficult to imagine, considering
how menacing they look when prowling the dark

00:16:58.630 --> 00:16:59.630
depths.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:03.700
We can be glad we’re not in the water…

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:05.950
Yet the air doesn’t seem safe either.

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:10.840
A flock of huge flying creatures soar just
behind our Hornets.

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:17.120
These are Red Deads, and with a wingspan of
almost 30 meters, or 100 feet, we might be

00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:19.410
in serious trouble.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:25.750
Yet despite a name that suggests aggressive
behavior, Red Deads are actually quite peaceful.

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:30.480
While from time to time they do have aggressive
fights with rivals — hence their nickname

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:34.410
— these majestic beings aren’t here to
pick a fight with us.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:39.860
Like dolphins accompanying Earth boats, Red
Deads are naturally inquisitive, and are flying

00:17:39.860 --> 00:17:44.280
alongside their new airborne ‘mates’ out
of sheer curiosity.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:49.300
Little else is known about these beautiful
red beings, although given their size, they

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:52.040
likely have no natural predators.

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:57.250
Soaring alongside this flock is a special
privilege…

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.500
Yet our voyage is not over yet.

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:04.760
Breaching near the surface are the final lifeforms
we’ll be observing on this tour.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:11.120
This is a family of Immanis Cetacea — the
largest living, moving creatures yet discovered

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:12.500
on this planet.

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:18.220
Dipping under the surface in an underwater
pod, we can see that like baleen whales, these

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:23.880
leviathans comb the oceans with their giant
mouths and eat everything that comes their

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:24.880
way.

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:30.261
Yet at over one hundred and ten meters, or
three-hundred and sixty feet in length, they

00:18:30.261 --> 00:18:34.140
make even the largest earth whales seem rather
small.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:40.380
In the unspoiled oceans of Aion, families
of Immanis Cetacea lead calm and peaceful

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:41.380
lives.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:46.260
And as incredible as it’s been to get close
to this species, we don’t want to disrupt

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:47.810
said lives.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:52.340
The time has come to head back…

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:56.820
Journeying through the ecosystems of Planet
Aion has been fascinating.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:02.590
You can discover more of the incredible vehicles
featured in this video in Christian Grajewski’s

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:06.800
‘Explorer’ artbook — which I’ve got
a link to in this video’s description.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:12.010
There are big plans for this universe: as
Grajewski is working with other creatives

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:16.680
on a novel and a screenplay — and is also
planning to release a second artbook that

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:21.260
will focus on the creatures and narrative
of this exciting world.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:25.670
To stay up to date on the project, follow
and support Christian Grajewski using the

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:27.050
links in the description.

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:30.780
I’m excited to see where the story goes
next…

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:34.130
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:38.890
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:43.600
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:20:03.730
See you in the next video.

